[
  {
    "pkheadingid": 803,
    "bookid": 53,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Definition",
    "headingPosition": 1,
    "headingid": 607,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 123071,
        "bookId": 53,
        "chapterId": 105,
        "headingId": 607,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Ganglioneuroma (Schwannian stroma&ndash;dominant) is the final stage of maturation of peripheral neuroblastic tumours of neural crest origin.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-10-09T12:55:44"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 804,
    "bookid": 53,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-O coding",
    "headingPosition": 2,
    "headingid": 608,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 123072,
        "bookId": 53,
        "chapterId": 105,
        "headingId": 608,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>9490/0 Ganglioneuroma</p>\r\n<p>9490/0 Maturing ganglioneuroma</p>\r\n<p>9490/0 Mature ganglioneuroma</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-10-09T12:55:44"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 805,
    "bookid": 53,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-11 coding",
    "headingPosition": 3,
    "headingid": 609,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 123073,
        "bookId": 53,
        "chapterId": 105,
        "headingId": 609,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>2A00.21 &amp; XH03L9 Mixed neuronal-glial tumours &amp; Ganglioneuroma</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-10-09T12:55:44"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 806,
    "bookid": 53,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Related terminology",
    "headingPosition": 4,
    "headingid": 610,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 123074,
        "bookId": 53,
        "chapterId": 105,
        "headingId": 610,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-10-09T12:55:44"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 807,
    "bookid": 53,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 611,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 123075,
        "bookId": 53,
        "chapterId": 105,
        "headingId": 611,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Maturing ganglioneuroma; mature ganglioneuroma</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-10-09T12:55:44"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 808,
    "bookid": 53,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 612,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 123076,
        "bookId": 53,
        "chapterId": 105,
        "headingId": 612,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Ganglioneuroma occurs in sites associated with the development of the sympathetic nervous system {11346873; 27465021; 29369484}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-10-09T12:55:44"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 809,
    "bookid": 53,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 613,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 123077,
        "bookId": 53,
        "chapterId": 105,
        "headingId": 613,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Most ganglioneuromas are incidental discoveries during imaging for unrelated events {25501841} and are usually non-functioning {27465021; 18375900}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-10-09T12:55:44"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 810,
    "bookid": 53,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 614,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 123078,
        "bookId": 53,
        "chapterId": 105,
        "headingId": 614,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Ganglioneuroma is a rare tumour; its incidence is unknown. In paediatric series, older children and adolescents are affected {29369484; 18375900; 19530234}. Adrenal ganglioneuroma is a rare incidentaloma in adults {25501841; 28506406}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-10-09T12:55:44"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 811,
    "bookid": 53,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 615,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 123079,
        "bookId": 53,
        "chapterId": 105,
        "headingId": 615,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-10-09T12:55:44"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 812,
    "bookid": 53,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 616,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 123080,
        "bookId": 53,
        "chapterId": 105,
        "headingId": 616,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Maturation from neuroblastoma to ganglioneuroma is supported by cross-talk between neural crest&ndash;derived neuroblastic tumour cells and Schwannian stromal cells. The latter are recruited by biologically favourable neuroblasts that release factors promoting Schwann cell proliferation and development {10421272; 15743800}. In return, Schwann cells may secrete antiproliferation and differentiation factors that are critical for neuronal differentiation {9788610}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-10-09T12:55:44"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 819,
    "bookid": 53,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 623,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 123081,
        "bookId": 53,
        "chapterId": 105,
        "headingId": 623,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Adrenal ganglioneuroma is a localized, firm, yellow mass without haemorrhagic or necrotic areas. Focal calcification may be present. It can encase large vessels and may compress but not invade other vital structures {28506406}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-10-09T12:55:44"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 813,
    "bookid": 53,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 617,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 123082,
        "bookId": 53,
        "chapterId": 105,
        "headingId": 617,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Ganglioneuroma contains abundant Schwann cells in sheets and fascicles organized and bundled by perineurial cells. Variable numbers of mature and maturing ganglion cells that differ in size and shape and may be multinucleated are distributed individually or in small clusters in the maturing subtype of ganglioneuroma. In the mature subtype of ganglioneuroma, all ganglion cells are mature and covered with satellite cells. In both subtypes, naked neuritic processes (neuropil) are not detected around the ganglion cells because they are immediately incorporated into the cytoplasm of Schwann cells {10421272}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-10-09T12:55:44"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 814,
    "bookid": 53,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 618,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 123083,
        "bookId": 53,
        "chapterId": 105,
        "headingId": 618,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Smears show a variable number of ganglion cells admixed with considerable cellular fibrillary Schwannian stroma {31231909}. There is no neuroblastoma.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-10-09T12:55:44"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 815,
    "bookid": 53,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 619,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 123084,
        "bookId": 53,
        "chapterId": 105,
        "headingId": 619,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-10-09T12:55:44"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 818,
    "bookid": 53,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 622,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 123085,
        "bookId": 53,
        "chapterId": 105,
        "headingId": 622,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Essential:</em> a dominant Schwannian stromal component; maturing and/or mature ganglion cells.</p>\r\n<p><em>Desirable:</em> no neuroblast clusters in neuropil (naked neurites); no neuroblastoma nodules.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-10-09T12:55:44"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 816,
    "bookid": 53,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 620,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 123086,
        "bookId": 53,
        "chapterId": 105,
        "headingId": 620,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>In the International Neuroblastoma Risk Group Staging System (INRGSS), ganglioneuromas are typically locoregional tumours of stage&nbsp;L1 or&nbsp;L2 {19047290; 19530234; 27465021; 29369484}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-10-09T12:55:44"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 817,
    "bookid": 53,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 621,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 123087,
        "bookId": 53,
        "chapterId": 105,
        "headingId": 621,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The International Neuroblastoma Pathology Classification (INPC) classifies both ganglioneuroma subtypes into the favourable histology group {11745303}. The prognosis is excellent, even if excision is incomplete {25501841; 27465021; 29369484}. Rarely, biopsy may miss a coexisting neuroblastoma nodule whose biological characteristics may alter behaviour (see <a style=\"cursor:pointer\" href=\"/chaptercontent/53/104\" target=\"_blank\">section </a><em>Ganglioneuroblastoma, nodular</em>), and therefore a disclaimer, such as &ldquo;Favourable histology based on review of limited material&rdquo;, should be added.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2024-10-09T12:55:44"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]